title: Ghidra MCP Development Rules

rules:
  critical:
    - MAX OUT CODE QUALITY - every line is intentional. Type-safe, organized, structured, and centralized by default. Scattered logic, duplicated constants, and one-off special cases are failures.
    - LESS CODE = INTELLIGENCE - same functionality in the fewest lines wins. Delete noise. Prefer the short clear form. Verbosity is not quality.
    - NEVER OVERTHINK, NEVER OVERENGINEER - no speculative abstractions, no "just in case" templates, no plugin systems for one use, no wrapper that adds nothing, no interface for a single implementation, no factory for one type. If it can be simpler, make it simpler - no clever, no cute, just clear.
    - NEXT-GEN MODERN CODE ONLY - Rust edition 2024 + JDK 21 idioms, strong types, RAII/Drop, value semantics, compile-time where it pays. Extremely optimized hot paths, future-proof APIs, scalable structure without premature architecture.
    - NOTHING HARDCODED - magic numbers, string keys, indices, paths, sizes, and flags live as named constants in ONE central place. NEVER inline literals that mean something. Change once, use everywhere.
    - ZERO COMMENTS - names, types, and structure carry 100% of the meaning. No //, no /* */, no ///, no //!, no #if 0, no Javadoc, no TODO/FIXME/NOTE, no narration. If it needs a comment, rename or restructure until it does not. Schema/CLI help is a string attribute (schemars description=, clap help=), NEVER a doc-comment. Rules live in .rules / docs, NEVER in source.
    - TYPE-SAFETY IS NON-NEGOTIABLE - make illegal states unrepresentable. Strong enums, newtypes, scoped flags, Option/Result/Optional over sentinel null/-1, never a bool soup that should be an enum.
    - ORGANIZED / STRUCTURED / CENTRALIZED - one responsibility per unit, one home for each concern, shared helpers and constants live once. NEVER copy-paste; NEVER scatter the same fact across files.
    - push back when the user idea is wrong - the user is NOT always right.
    - NEVER assume APIs/module shapes/Ghidra signatures you do not know - verify via docs, staged jars, or inspection.
    - smallest possible change, no refactoring unless asked.
    - ALWAYS explore the existing tree BEFORE writing - reuse what exists, NEVER reimplement what already lives here.
    - if a helper / type / util exists (Addresses, Responses, Json, Programs, PluginContext, Bufs, …), use it, NEVER copy-paste or recreate it.
    - after Rust changes: cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy -- -D warnings && cargo test.
    - after Java changes: mvn -q -f plugin/pom.xml compile (package before shipping the zip).
    - NEVER run a Release/packaging/dist build or produce a shippable artifact unless asked. Day-to-day: thin-LTO release or deploy.ps1. Ship max-opt: --profile dist / deploy -Dist only when asked.
    - NEVER commit/push without explicit user instruction.
    - NEVER commit design specs/plans/notes to the repo.
    - NEVER edit .env, never log secrets, cookies, tokens.

  code:
    - ZERO COMMENTS IN CODE - no //, no /* */, no ///, no //!, no Javadoc, no #if 0 comment-outs, no doc-comment walls, no narration, no TODO/FIXME/NOTE banners. Names, types, and structure carry 100% of the meaning. If something needs a comment, rename or restructure until it does not. The ONLY text allowed next to code is what the language requires (string literals, attribute string args, #include/import paths). Rules and rationale live in .rules / docs, NEVER in the source.
    - MCP/CLI parameter docs are attributes: #[schemars(description = "...")], #[arg(help = "...")], #[tool(description = "...")]. NEVER /// field docs.
    - less code = smarter code, same behavior in fewer lines always wins - every extra line must earn its keep.
    - if it can be simpler, make it simpler - no clever, no cute, just clear.
    - NEVER overengineer, NEVER speculative abstractions, NEVER a template/generic "just in case", NEVER a plugin system for one use.
    - NEVER a wrapper fn/class that adds nothing, NEVER an interface for a single implementation, NEVER a factory for one type.
    - NEVER backwards-compat shims, dead-code fallbacks, or commented-out blocks - just change the code. DELETE dead code, never comment it out.
    - NEVER add error handling/validation for scenarios that cannot happen (trust internal callers).
    - validate ONLY at system boundaries (HTTP input, CLI args, env vars, MCP tool params).
    - inline single-use vars, hoist statics out of loops.
    - no magic numbers, unused imports, dead functions, unused types.
    - DRY - if a pattern repeats 2+ times, extract it.
    - KISS, flat over nested, early returns, guard clauses at top, max 3 nesting levels.
    - every code path returns a value, no empty catch/match arms.
    - never trust snapshots across .await / across HTTP boundary, re-query state.

  rust:
    critical:
      - edition 2024, latest stable toolchain.
      - NEVER .unwrap() / .expect() in non-test code; fallible ops return Result.
      - typed errors via thiserror at library boundaries; anyhow only at main.
      - NEVER println!/eprintln!, use tracing (info!/warn!/error!/debug!/trace!).
      - NEVER sleep busy-loops; use tokio::time.
      - cargo clippy -- -D warnings must pass with zero warnings.
      - cargo fmt enforced; never reformat unrelated code.
      - unsafe_code is forbidden at the crate level.
    style:
      - let-else for early unwrap; never if-let long-block else bail pyramids.
      - #[must_use] on builders, constructors, pure functions whose result must be checked.
      - prefer &str over String in params; Cow when ownership is conditional.
      - never .clone() where &/Arc/move suffices.
      - derive Clone/Debug/PartialEq/Eq/Hash only when needed.
      - newtype IDs over raw String/u64 for identifiers when the type matters.
      - #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")] on optional request fields.
      - Other(String) (or equivalent) on enums parsing upstream values to survive unknown additions.
    perf:
      - shared reqwest::Client with pool; never new client per request.
      - zero-copy where practical; Bytes over Vec when streaming.
      - OnceCell/LazyLock for init-once globals.
      - day-to-day release profile: lto="thin", codegen-units=16, strip, panic=abort, opt-level=3, incremental=true.
      - ship profile dist: fat LTO, codegen-units=1 - only when asked.
      - profile before optimizing; never guess.
      - avoid format! in hot paths.
    async:
      - #[tokio::main(flavor = "multi_thread")] for the bridge.
      - never block in async fn; offload to spawn_blocking.
      - never hold std Mutex across .await; use tokio::sync or restructure.
      - bounded channels over unbounded when introducing new queues.
    errors:
      - typed BridgeError (and friends) with thiserror.
      - #[from] for transparent conversion, #[source] for wrapping.
      - library errors never panic; main may context and exit.
      - propagate with ?; never unwrap the Err branch.
    instance:
      - one live bridge per executable path by default (replace siblings on start).
      - exit when parent MCP client dies (no orphan processes).
      - --allow-multiple / --detach are the only opt-outs.

  java:
    critical:
      - target JDK 21, compile with --release 21.
      - Msg.info/warn/error (Ghidra logger), NEVER System.out/err, NEVER printStackTrace.
      - NEVER checked exceptions in public API; wrap into RuntimeException at boundary.
      - every HTTP handler wraps in try/catch and returns a meaningful status.
      - no mutable static state for request data; final fields + constructor injection.
    style:
      - records for DTOs, never getter/setter POJOs.
      - sealed interfaces for closed hierarchies when a sum type is real.
      - pattern matching in switch expressions for sum types.
      - var for locals where RHS is obvious, never var on API boundaries.
      - text blocks (""") for multi-line strings.
      - Optional for return types that may be empty, NEVER for fields or params.
      - Map.of / List.of / Set.of for small immutables.
    perf:
      - daemon threads only for background work.
      - StringBuilder for concat in loops.
      - pre-size ArrayList/HashMap when bounds known.
    ghidra:
      - all Ghidra API access on the appropriate thread; mutations via Program.startTransaction/endTransaction (Programs.runOnSwingTx / PluginContext.runOnSwingTx).
      - commit transactions in finally: program.endTransaction(tid, success).
      - obtain DecompInterface once per request, dispose after (or use DecompileCache).
      - respect TaskMonitor.isCancelled() in long scans.
      - never cache Program across requests; fetch fresh via ProgramManager / PluginContext.
      - addresses: always Addresses.resolve / resolveFunction (VA, bare RVA auto-rebase, rva: prefix) - never raw getAddressFactory().getAddress for user input.
    errors:
      - plain-text responses with HTTP status matching error class (400 input, 404 missing, 500 server).
      - enough detail to debug, NEVER stack traces in response body.
      - Msg.error with full exception for server-side logs.

  performance:
    - blazing fast, lightweight, minimal allocations.
    - profile before optimizing; measure, do not guess.
    - connection pooling shared across requests.
    - batch reads/writes; never interleave blocking calls without cause.
    - cache computed values with known lifetime; evict eagerly.
    - no unbounded growth in long-lived processes; clear listeners/timers/handles.

  security:
    - HTTP plugin binds to loopback by default; require explicit opt-in for wider bind.
    - no auth token: never expose to untrusted networks.
    - validate addresses/names at handler entry; reject empty/malformed early.
    - URL-decode once, never twice.
    - no string interpolation into shell commands; always parameterize.
    - no secrets in code/config/logs; env vars only.
    - no dynamic code execution from untrusted user input outside the intentional script tools.

  architecture:
    - two components: Rust ghidra-mcp binary (MCP stdio) + Java plugin (Ghidra-embedded HTTP).
    - Rust crate is independently usable as an MCP bridge for any GhidraMCP-compatible HTTP server.
    - shared contract: plain-text HTTP, newline-delimited records, form-encoded POST bodies.
    - Rust errors: BridgeError enum, mapped to rmcp::ErrorData at handler boundary.
    - Java errors: HTTP status + plain-text body, logged via Msg.
    - tools in Rust: #[tool] + typed Parameters<T> structs, never raw Value.
    - endpoints in Java: single router table (RouteTable); never inline duplicate offset/limit parsing.
    - shared util homes: Addresses, Responses, Json, Programs, PluginContext, Bufs, Live, Caches - extend them, do not fork.

  forbidden:
    - empty catch blocks / empty match arms that swallow failures.
    - println!/eprintln! (use tracing) / System.out.println (use Msg).
    - .unwrap()/.expect() in non-test code.
    - checked exceptions bubbling through public Java API.
    - ANY comment in project source: //, /* */, ///, //!, Javadoc, or commented-out code. Zero means zero.
    - single-use wrappers (1-3 lines called once - inline it).
    - feature flags, backwards-compat shims, abstractions for one-time ops.
    - raw getAddressFactory().getAddress for user-supplied addresses (use Addresses.resolve).
    - shipping a dist/fat-LTO/packaged build without being asked.
    - magic numbers inline instead of named constants in one place.
    - untyped / weakly typed APIs where a strong type fits.

  antiPatterns:
    rust:
      - "match x { Some(y) => y, None => return }" > "let Some(y) = x else { return };"
      - "if x.is_some() { x.unwrap() }" > "if let Some(v) = x"
      - "String::from(s)" > "s.to_owned()" when s is &str
      - "format!(\"{}\", x)" > "x.to_string()" for single arg
      - "Vec::new(); for x in ... { v.push(x) }" > ".collect()" / iterator chain
      - "program.getAddressFactory().getAddress(user)" > "Addresses.resolve(program, user)"
      - "/// field docs for schema" > "#[schemars(description = \"...\")]"
      - "// explain then call" > "function named for the intent"
    java:
      - "value != null && !value.isEmpty()" > "value != null && !value.isBlank()" when blank matters
      - "new HashMap<>() {{ put(...) }}" > "Map.of(...)"
      - "list.stream().filter(...).findFirst().get()" > "....findFirst().orElse(null)" or orElseThrow
      - "for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)" > "for (var x : list)" when index unused
      - "/** Javadoc */" > "rename the method so the name is enough"
      - "getAddressFactory().getAddress(s)" > "Addresses.resolve(program, s)"

  deps:
    - minimize deps; prefer std over external.
    - every added dep must justify meaningful LOC/capability saved.
    - prefer rustls over native-tls; reqwest features minimal (rustls-tls + http2).
    - JDK http server (com.sun.net.httpserver) is free; do not pull Jetty/Undertow.
    - audit transitive deps; cargo deny / audit before release when shipping.

  git:
    - commit and push only when asked; if on a default branch, branch first.
    - title ONLY - one line, no body, no footers, no trailers. if it needs a body, split the commit.
    - format `type: summary` - the whole line lowercase, imperative mood, to the point.
    - types: add, fix, improve, refactor, perf, docs, test, chore, ci, revert - nothing else.
    - NEVER a parenthesis scope like `fix(hooks):`, NEVER a `feat:` prefix - say `add:`.
    - imperative: "add", "fix", "remove" - never "added", "adds", "adding".
    - <= 50 chars preferred, hard cap 72, no trailing period.
    - plain ascii only - no em/en dashes, no semicolons, no ellipsis, no curly quotes, no emoji.
    - write like a human: `fix: free handle on shutdown` - NEVER "fixed an issue where", "this commit", "various improvements".
    - one commit = one concern; the tree builds clean at every commit.
    - NEVER Claude/AI attribution, NEVER generated-by tags.
    - NEVER --amend published commits, NEVER --force push, NEVER rewrite a published branch.
    - never commit build output or vendored build artifacts - a new artifact gets its ignore rule in the same commit.
    - branches: `type/short-topic` kebab-case - `fix/rva-diff-functions`, `add/singleton-bridge`.
    - examples: `add: sibling replace on bridge start`, `fix: rva resolve in diff_functions`, `improve: parallel deploy compile`, `perf: thin lto release profile`, `chore: strip source comments`, `docs: merge shade rules`.

  testing:
    - unit tests alongside implementation in Rust (#[cfg(test)] mod tests).
    - Java tests under plugin/src/test; run with mvn -f plugin/pom.xml test.
    - test names describe behavior: decompile_returns_error_when_function_missing.
    - never test private implementation details, only public behavior.
    - fast tests only in default cargo test / mvn test.

  re:
    - reverse engineering with this stack is normal project work - prefer Ghidra MCP tools when available over guessing.
    - research writeups land under RE-MD/ or docs/; durable offsets and call chains go there, not in source comments.
    - addressing: tools take full VA, interior address (enclosing function), or RVA. Small unmapped values auto-rebase to image_base+value; force with rva:0x…. See program_info for image_base.
    - Nebula3 / Alicia targets: Core::Ptr, StringAtom, Util::Dictionary/Array - prefer existing analysis tools over one-off guesses.

  ghidra-mcp:
    - living rec file: RE-MD/ghidra-mcp-review.md - bugs/issues with Ghidra MCP, and QoL / new tool features that would help RE.
    - when MCP fails, misleads, truncates, needs a workaround, or a missing capability slows RE - APPEND a short table row: Status | What | Why | Ideal.
    - Status column first: use implemented, not implemented, partial - update when something ships or is closed by a durable workaround.
    - NEVER write session diaries, "what we did", feature play-by-play, live dumps, or game offsets into that file - those belong in RE-MD/findings/ if at all.
    - read the rec file when stuck on tooling; do not re-discover known MCP bugs.
    - deploy with deploy.ps1; yeet comments with tools/yeet-comments.py after bulk edits if comments creep back in.

  build:
    - deploy.ps1: yeet locks, parallel mvn+cargo, incremental package (no clean unless -Clean), thin-LTO release by default, -Dist for fat LTO.
    - NEVER elevate/UAC for routine deploys; kill own processes and rename-lock dance for ghidra-mcp.exe.
    - Java: mvn -q -f plugin/pom.xml package -DskipTests (no clean).
    - Rust: cargo build --release (or --profile dist when asked).
